<%
  primary_host = AppHost.primary_host
  server_ip = AppHost.server_ip
  cname_name = Current.organization.custom_domain.presence || "app"
%>

<p class="text-sm text-base-content/60">
  Add <span class="font-medium">one</span> of these records at your domain provider (the
  place you registered or manage DNS for the domain) - whichever matches how you'll use
  the domain. You don't need both.
</p>

<div class="card border border-base-300">
  <div class="card-body p-0">
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="table table-sm">
        <thead class="text-sm font-semibold text-base-content bg-base-200">
          <tr class="border-b border-base-300">
            <th class="w-24">Type</th>
            <th>Name</th>
            <th>Points to</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-base-300 hover:bg-base-200">
            <td class="font-mono text-xs font-medium">CNAME</td>
            <td><%= render "shared/copyable_value", value: cname_name %></td>
            <td><%= render "shared/copyable_value", value: primary_host %></td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hover:bg-base-200">
            <td class="font-mono text-xs font-medium">A</td>
            <td><%= render "shared/copyable_value", value: "@" %></td>
            <td><%= render "shared/copyable_value", value: server_ip %></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="text-sm text-base-content/60 space-y-2">
  <p>
    <span class="font-medium text-base-content/80">Use CNAME</span> if your domain is a
    subdomain, e.g. <code>book.yourclinic.com</code> or <code>app.yourclinic.com</code>.
    A CNAME record aliases your subdomain to <code><%= primary_host %></code>, so it always
    follows wherever that host currently points - no changes needed on your end if our
    server IP ever changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="font-medium text-base-content/80">Use A</span> if your domain is a bare/root
    domain, e.g. <code>yourclinic.com</code> with no subdomain (the "Name" field is
    <code>@</code>, meaning the root of the zone). An A record points directly at our
    server's IP address. Root domains can't use a plain CNAME per the DNS spec, which is
    why the root needs an A record instead - if your DNS provider offers an ALIAS or ANAME
    record type, that works too and behaves like the CNAME option above.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once the record is saved, DNS changes can take anywhere from a few minutes up to
    24-48 hours to propagate depending on your provider. The status badge above polls
    automatically and flips to <span class="font-medium text-base-content/80">Ready</span>
    as soon as it sees your domain resolving here - you don't need to refresh the page.
    A TLS/SSL certificate is then requested and installed automatically the first time a
    visitor hits the domain over HTTPS; there's nothing to upload or configure yourself.
  </p>
</div>
